DESCRIPTION:Event Details:\n\nIn surfacing\, Patty Chang engages the ocean 
 as a physical\, geopolitical\, and symbolic space\, inviting you to consid
 er the work of collection and recollection. Chang’s multimedia sculptura
 l practice brings together archaeological remains from Point Alones Chines
 e Fishing Village (now the site of Stanford’s Hopkins Marine Station)\, 
 fish specimens from Harvard’s Museum of Comparative Zoology\, and archiv
 al materials documenting the lives of Chinese fisher families on the Monte
 rey Peninsula.  In a critical encounter with the scientific enterprise of 
 survey and collection\, Chang surfaces the mysterious intimacies\, forgott
 en collaborators\, and unrecorded histories that haunt the Pacific and its
  coast.\n\nPatty Chang: surfacing culminates a year-long residency through
  the Denning Visiting Artist program\, during which Chang was embedded at 
 Stanford’s Hopkins Marine Station. The exhibition’s central sculptural
  work is a porcelain massage table that was originally commissioned for th
 e Metropolitan Museum of Art’s exhibition Monstrous Beauty: A Feminist R
 evision of Chinoiserie and now travels to Stanford to take new form as a s
 urface upon which to dry squid. This act of transmutation connects histori
 es of the ocean\, labor\, and racial and gendered violence: the transpacif
 ic migration of porcelain and people\; the scientific collection of marine
  specimens\; the labor of fishing and drying squid\; and the 2021 Atlanta 
 Spa Shootings.  It is a project of grief\, memorial\, rage\, and disobedia
 nce—an invitation to think with the deep\, and to watch for what begins 
 to rise.\n\nOn view: September 17\, 2026 - February 8\, 2027\nAnderson Col
 lection\, 314 Lomita Dr\nGallery Hours: Thursday-Monday\, 11am–5pm\nCura
 ted by Kelsey Chen and Marci Kwon \n\nACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\nPatty Chang: surf
 acing is the first solo artist commission by Stanford’s Asian American A
 rt Initiative.  It is organized by the Anderson Collection at Stanford Uni
 versity and co-curated by Kelsey Chen\, interdisciplinary theorist\, artis
 t\, PhD Candidate in Modern Thought & Literature\, and Marci Kwon\, Associ
 ate Professor of Art & Art History and Marta Sutton Weeks Faculty Scholar 
 in the Humanities.  The exhibition is made possible with support from the 
 Robert H. N. Ho Family Foundation Global\, a Humanities Seed Grant from St
 anford Public Humanities\, the Stanford Arts Roberta Bowman Denning Visiti
 ng Artist Fund\, and generous support for programs and operations from Wil
 liam Reller & Kristin E. Klint\, Anderson Student Engagement Fund\, and mu
 seum members. We extend special thanks to the staff of Hopkins Marine Stat
 ion\, Quock Mui Foundation\, and the Point Alones Chinese Fishing Village 
 descendant community.\n\n \n\nVISITOR INFORMATION\n\nThe Anderson Collecti
 on at Stanford University is located at 314 Lomita Drive\, Stanford\, CA 9
 4305\, on the Stanford campus\, adjacent to the Cantor Arts Center. Patty 
 Chang: surfacing is on view in the Wisch Family Gallery. The museum is ope
 n Monday and Thursday–Sunday\, 11 a.m.–5 p.m.\, and closed Tuesday and
  Wednesday. Visitor parking is available in designated areas\, with contac
 tless payment managed through ParkMobile.\n\nAlternatively\, take Caltrain
  to the Palo Alto Transit Center and connect to the free Stanford Margueri
 te Shuttle. The exhibition is open to Stanford affiliates and the general 
 public. Admission is free\, and advance reservations are not required.\n\n
 Connect with the Anderson Collection! Subscribe to our mailing list and fo
 llow us on Instagram and Facebook.
GEO:37.433768;-122.170791
LOCATION:Anderson Collection\, Wisch Family Gallery
SUMMARY:Patty Chang: surfacing
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.stanford.edu/event/patty-chang-surfacing
CATEGORIES:Exhibition
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
